Lab 1: Secure Environment Setup for Advanced ML
Objective
Configure a secure Linux-based environment optimized for advanced machine learning tasks, set up Python, Jupyter, and essential ML libraries.

Prerequisites
Ubuntu-based machine (or any Linux distribution)

Basic Linux administration skills (sudo access)

Steps
Step 1: Install and Configure the Linux Environment for ML
Install the latest updates for Ubuntu:

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
Install essential development tools:
These tools will allow you to compile packages that are necessary for ML libraries.

sudo apt install build-essential dkms curl git wget -y
Add necessary PPAs and repositories:

Add the Python 3.9 repository (or any other latest Python version).
sudo add-apt-repository ppa:deadsnakes/ppa
sudo apt update
sudo apt install python3.9 python3.9-venv python3.9-dev python3-pip -y
Secure the environment:

Set up ufw (Uncomplicated Firewall) to limit unwanted access to your system.
sudo apt install ufw -y
sudo ufw allow OpenSSH  # Allow SSH
sudo ufw enable
sudo ufw status
Disable root access to enhance security (if necessary):
sudo passwd -l root
Enable fail2ban to prevent brute-force attacks:
sudo apt install fail2ban -y
sudo systemctl enable fail2ban
sudo systemctl start fail2ban
Step 2: Set Up Python and Virtual Environments
Install Python 3.9 and pip:

sudo apt install python3-pip -y
Install and set up a virtual environment to isolate your ML projects:

python3 -m venv ml_env
source ml_env/bin/activate
This will activate a clean Python environment where you can install dependencies without interfering with the system Python.
Step 3: Install Jupyter Notebook and Necessary Libraries
Install Jupyter Notebook:

pip install jupyter
After installation, you can start Jupyter Notebook by running:

jupyter notebook
This will start Jupyter Notebook and open it in your default web browser (typically at http://localhost:8888).
Install essential Machine Learning libraries:

Install popular ML libraries like NumPy, Pandas, Scikit-learn, TensorFlow, and PyTorch:
pip install numpy pandas scikit-learn matplotlib seaborn tensorflow pytorch
Additional libraries for deep learning:
pip install keras opencv-python pillow
Step 4: Verify the Installation
Verify Python and Jupyter installation:

Check Python version:
python --version
Run Jupyter Notebook:
jupyter notebook
Verify ML libraries:

Open a Jupyter notebook and test basic functionality:
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import tensorflow as tf
import torch

print("Libraries loaded successfully!")
Step 5: Secure Jupyter Notebook
To enhance the security of your Jupyter Notebook server, use a password and enable HTTPS for secure connections.

Set a password for Jupyter:
Run the following command to generate a configuration file:

jupyter notebook --generate-config
Set a password for Jupyter:

jupyter notebook password
Configure Jupyter to use HTTPS:

You can set up SSL by generating certificates or using an existing one.

Modify the ~/.jupyter/jupyter_notebook_config.py to add:

c.NotebookApp.certfile = u'/path/to/your/certificate.pem'
c.NotebookApp.keyfile = u'/path/to/your/privatekey.pem'
c.NotebookApp.ip = '0.0.0.0'
c.NotebookApp.open_browser = False
c.NotebookApp.port = 8888
Step 6: Test the Setup
Run Jupyter Notebook:
After configuration, run:

jupyter notebook
Access Jupyter via https://localhost:8888 (or replace with your server IP if remote).

Test ML Libraries:
Open a new notebook and run the following test code:

import tensorflow as tf
print(f"TensorFlow version: {tf.__version__}")
print(f"PyTorch version: {torch.__version__}")
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Configured a secure Linux environment for advanced machine learning.

Set up Python, Jupyter, and essential ML libraries (TensorFlow, PyTorch, Scikit-learn).

Secured the Jupyter Notebook server with password protection and HTTPS.

Tested the environment to ensure everything is working correctly for advanced machine learning tasks.

This setup provides you with a secure environment optimized for advanced machine learning projects, ensuring your data and models are handled in a safe and isolated environment.
